Looked at the app: clustered Express (4 workers, no shared memory) behind a load balancer, Postgres for data, Redis already shared across every worker/box for sessions. That shapes the design.

**Key by `client_id`, not IP.** The auth middleware (`routes/api.js:9-19`) already resolves `req.client.client_id` from the API key before any route runs. The comment there says multiple clients can share an address (NAT) and one client can call from many addresses — so IP is the wrong axis. Rate limit on `client_id`.

**State has to live in Redis, not process memory.** `app.js:5-7` explicitly notes each of the 4 workers has its own heap with nothing shared, and `redis.js:3-4` notes Redis is "the only piece of state the workers have in common" across every worker on every box. An in-process limiter (e.g. `express-rate-limit`'s default store) would let each worker enforce the limit independently — the client would get 4x (or more, across boxes) the intended budget. The limiter has to check a shared counter in the existing Redis instance.

**Algorithm: token bucket per client, stored in Redis.** Fixed windows let a client burst 2x the limit at the window boundary; a token bucket (or sliding-window log) smooths that out and still allows short bursts. Implement it as a single Lua script run via `EVAL` so the read-modify-write (check tokens, refill by elapsed time, decrement, set TTL) is atomic — otherwise concurrent requests from the same hammering client, landing on different workers, race on the same Redis key and both pass.

Sketch (illustrative, not written to a file):

```lua
-- KEYS[1] = "ratelimit:{client_id}"
-- ARGV[1] = now (ms), ARGV[2] = rate (tokens/sec), ARGV[3] = burst, ARGV[4] = cost
local bucket = redis.call("HMGET", KEYS[1], "tokens", "ts")
local tokens = tonumber(bucket[1]) or tonumber(ARGV[3])
local ts = tonumber(bucket[2]) or tonumber(ARGV[1])
local elapsed = math.max(0, tonumber(ARGV[1]) - ts) / 1000
tokens = math.min(tonumber(ARGV[3]), tokens + elapsed * tonumber(ARGV[2]))
local allowed = tokens >= tonumber(ARGV[4])
if allowed then tokens = tokens - tonumber(ARGV[4]) end
redis.call("HMSET", KEYS[1], "tokens", tokens, "ts", ARGV[1])
redis.call("EXPIRE", KEYS[1], 60)
return { allowed and 1 or 0, tokens }
```

```js
// middleware, inserted after auth resolves req.client, before route handlers
async function rateLimit(req, res, next) {
  const { rate, burst } = plans[req.client.plan]; // per-plan limits
  const [allowed, remaining] = await redis.client.evalRateLimit(req.client.client_id, rate, burst, 1);
  res.set("X-RateLimit-Limit", burst);
  res.set("X-RateLimit-Remaining", Math.floor(remaining));
  if (!allowed) {
    res.set("Retry-After", "1");
    return res.status(429).end();
  }
  next();
}
```

**Placement in the chain matters.** It goes right after the auth middleware resolves `req.client` (needs the client identity) and before any route touches Postgres — otherwise a hammering client still exhausts DB connections even while being "rate limited" downstream. Worth noting: the auth middleware itself does a Postgres `SELECT` on every single request (`routes/api.js:12-15`), so a client hammering `/v1` degrades everyone by saturating the Postgres pool at the auth step, before the rate limiter even runs. That lookup should be cached in Redis (key → `client_id`/`plan`, short TTL) so the hammering traffic gets absorbed by Redis instead of Postgres.

**Failure mode: fail open on Redis errors.** Redis is now a dependency of every request. If it errors or times out, don't 500 the whole API — log/alert and let the request through, otherwise a Redis blip takes down `/v1` for every client instead of just throttling one. This is a deliberate tradeoff (protects availability over strict enforcement during outages).

**Other things worth building in:**
- Per-plan limits (`req.client.plan` is already resolved), not a single global number.
- A per-client *concurrency* cap in addition to rate, so a client can't dodge the rate limit by holding many slow requests open at once (relevant for `POST /reports` if report generation is expensive).
- Metrics/logging on 429s by `client_id` so you can see who's getting throttled and reach out, rather than just silently rejecting.
- Defense in depth: a coarse limit at the load balancer/gateway layer catches raw volumetric abuse before it even reaches the Node processes, but the per-client_id Redis limiter is the load-bearing piece since the LB only sees IPs.

